var BANK_PARTS = BANK_PARTS || [];
BANK_PARTS.push([
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"The city licensing board has approved every application for a rooftop apiary that included a signed waiver from the building's insurer. The Hale Street co-op's application includes such a waiver. A member concludes that the board will approve the Hale Street application.","q":"The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument","c":["treats a feature of the applications the board has approved as though that feature ensured a future approval","assumes that the board has never approved an apiary application that lacked an insurer's waiver","infers that the co-op caused the waiver to be signed merely because the application and the waiver are mentioned together","draws a conclusion about every rooftop in the city from a single insurer's signature","takes for granted that a waiver sufficient for a rooftop hive is required for a hive kept at street level"],"a":0,"e":"The premise says only that approved applications had waivers, so a waiver was present in past successes. The member treats that feature as enough to guarantee the Hale Street approval, which does not follow. The attractive choice — assumes that the board has never approved an apiary application that lacked an insurer's waiver — loses because it restates the premise. \"Every approved application included a waiver\" already means none was approved without one. The error is reading that record as a guarantee, not quietly assuming the record."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Causal reversal","d":3,"s":"Cities with higher rates of bicycle commuting also have more miles of protected bike lanes. A columnist concludes that the lanes produce the higher commuting rates.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["fails to establish that protected lanes are the only factor that can raise a city's bicycle-commuting rate","overlooks the possibility that a high rate of bicycle commuting led cities to build the protected lanes","treats a property of a city's commuting rate as though it were a property of each commuter","rejects the columnist's conclusion by appealing to the columnist's motive for praising construction","bases the comparison on cities that have not yet built any protected lanes"],"a":1,"e":"The columnist moves from a correlation to the claim that the lanes produce the commuting rates. Commuting could instead have prompted the construction, which would reverse the stated cause. The attractive choice — fails to establish that protected lanes are the only factor that can raise a city's bicycle-commuting rate — loses because the columnist claims the lanes produce these higher rates, not that nothing else can raise a rate. A second cause of commuting would not, by itself, show that the lanes played no role."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":3,"s":"If the conservation lab humidifies the vellum gallery, the illuminated pages will not cockle. The lab will not humidify the gallery this month. Therefore, the illuminated pages will cockle.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["concludes that the pages will cockle merely because humidifying and cockling are discussed in the same plan","assumes that every gallery that humidifies its air stores illuminated pages","treats a step that would prevent cockling as though it were the only way to prevent cockling","infers that the lab as a whole will fail because one gallery is left unhumidified","takes for granted that the pages cockled in an earlier month under the same settings"],"a":2,"e":"Humidifying is given as sufficient to prevent cockling. Refusing to humidify rules out that one safeguard, and the argument treats the damage as then inevitable. Some other control could still keep the pages flat. The attractive choice — concludes that the pages will cockle merely because humidifying and cockling are discussed in the same plan — loses because there is no coincidence being read as a cause. The argument denies the antecedent of a conditional and draws the affirmative result. That is a different error from pairing two topics."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each of the twelve stained-glass panels proposed for the chapel transept weighs less than the hoist in the transept is rated to lift. The architect concludes that the hoist can lift the twelve panels in a single load.","q":"The argument's reasoning is questionable because it","c":["assumes that the hoist cannot lift any panel unless it can lift all twelve at once","draws the conclusion from one panel that is heavier than the other eleven","treats the architect's preference for stained glass as evidence about the hoist's rating","infers that a combined load has a property from the fact that each panel in the load has that property","fails to consider that the chapel might leave the hoist unused on installation day"],"a":3,"e":"Each panel is under the rating. The combined weight can still exceed the rating. The architect assigns the panels' individual property to the load taken together. The attractive choice — assumes that the hoist cannot lift any panel unless it can lift all twelve at once — loses because the architect concludes that one combined lift is possible, not that separate lifts are impossible. The necessary-condition claim runs in the opposite direction from the inference."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Whole-to-part","d":2,"s":"The competition jury praised the string ensemble's nocturne recording for its rhythmic variety. Therefore, the first movement, which is part of that recording, itself has rhythmic variety.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["assumes that a recording can win praise only if each movement is submitted as its own file","infers that the ensemble caused the praise merely because the jury and the ensemble are mentioned together","bases the conclusion on judges who have heard only the first movement","treats \"variety\" in the jury's praise as a different virtue from \"variety\" in the conclusion","takes a merit of the whole recording to be a merit of one movement inside it"],"a":4,"e":"Variety can come from differences among movements. A nocturne can be rhythmically varied as a whole while a single movement stays metrically plain. The argument assigns the whole recording's merit to one part. The attractive choice — assumes that a recording can win praise only if each movement is submitted as its own file — loses because the argument moves from the whole performance to a movement inside it. It does not claim that separate files are required before praise is allowed."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Dr. Ibarra, a cardiologist, presents hospital figures showing that the clinic's nighttime discharge rate rose after the new billing software went in, and argues from those figures that the software should be suspended until the discharges are reviewed. A board member replies that Ibarra's sibling sells a competing product, and concludes that the figures do not warrant a suspension.","q":"The board member's reasoning is flawed because it","c":["sets aside a recommendation grounded in the discharge figures by appealing to the recommender's family interest","overlooks the possibility that the billing software shortened the time patients waited for discharge papers","treats a condition sufficient for suspending the software as though it were necessary","infers that every clinic using the software has a rising nighttime discharge rate","assumes that hospital figures cannot describe a rate that changes after a software installation"],"a":0,"e":"The only reason offered for rejecting the figures is the sibling's business. A family interest may be a reason to look harder at the figures. It does not show that the figures fail to warrant a suspension. The attractive choice — overlooks the possibility that the billing software shortened the time patients waited for discharge papers — loses because a benefit of the software might matter to a different question, whether suspension is wise all things considered. The board member's stated move is to discard the figures because of who is related to the speaker, not to weigh paperwork speed."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Listeners who called the classical station during a pledge drive were asked whether the station should drop its morning news headlines. Four-fifths of the callers said yes. The program director concludes that most of the station's listeners want the headlines dropped.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["fails to consider that some callers who want the headlines dropped still value the music that follows the headlines","takes the views of listeners who chose to call during a pledge drive to represent the listeners who did not call","assumes that minutes now used for headlines cannot be used for music unless the headlines are dropped","treats the director's own taste in news copy as proof of how listeners voted","infers that the station's donations will fall because the question was asked during a pledge drive"],"a":1,"e":"The callers selected themselves during a pledge drive. Their four-fifths vote is evidence about those callers. The conclusion is about listeners generally, including people who never called. The attractive choice — fails to consider that some callers who want the headlines dropped still value the music that follows the headlines — loses because whether callers also value the music is a further preference of people already in the sample. It does not repair or describe the step from that sample to listeners who stayed silent."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Equivocation","d":3,"s":"The carriage contract requires the shipper to provide a \"secure\" container, and in that contract the word means a container that staff cannot open without a recorded key. The customs notice calls the container secure, meaning only that it arrived with its seal unbroken. The broker concludes that the shipper has satisfied the contract.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["assumes that a container meets the contract only when a customs officer has opened and repacked it","infers that the shipper caused the seal to stay unbroken merely because the notice and the contract use one adjective","treats the customs notice's use of \"secure\" as if it established the different requirement in the contract","draws a conclusion about every container on the vessel from a notice about this container","takes for granted that an unbroken seal is a property of each item packed inside the container"],"a":2,"e":"The contract's \"secure\" is about keyed access. The notice's \"secure\" is about an unbroken seal. The broker treats the notice as proof that the contract's requirement was met. The attractive choice — assumes that a container meets the contract only when a customs officer has opened and repacked it — loses because the argument never makes an inspection a requirement. It slides between two meanings of the same word, and an inspection rule is a third condition nobody invoked."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The festival director argues: either the concert is moved indoors, or the entire performance will be rained out. The concert will not be moved indoors. Therefore, the entire performance will be rained out.","q":"The argument is flawed because it","c":["denies that moving the concert indoors would keep the performance from being rained out","bases the forecast on audience members who have attended only indoor concerts","concludes that the director caused the rain merely because the plan and the weather are discussed together","relies on an either-or claim that ignores ways the performance could continue outdoors without being entirely lost","takes a limit that applies to the whole festival season to apply to this one concert"],"a":3,"e":"Granting the either-or, canceling the indoor move would yield a rainout. The either-or itself is unsupported. A delay, a canopy, or a shortened set could avoid both a move and a total loss. The attractive choice — denies that moving the concert indoors would keep the performance from being rained out — loses because the director uses the indoor option as a way to avoid a rainout. The argument does not deny that link. The unsupported step is the claim that those two options exhaust the possibilities."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"At a regional blood bank, donations from first-time donors fell from 45 percent of all donations in the spring quarter to 30 percent in the summer quarter. The director concludes that the bank received fewer donations from first-time donors in the summer.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["assumes that a person cannot be a first-time donor in the spring and a repeat donor in the summer","treats the director's recruiting goal as the reason the percentage changed","infers that every summer donor had given blood in an earlier quarter","bases the conclusion on donors who gave at a single afternoon drive","infers a drop in the number of first-time donations from a drop in their share of donations, without a comparison of the two quarters' totals"],"a":4,"e":"A smaller share of a larger summer total can be a larger number. The director reads the percentage drop as a drop in count, and no total is supplied. The attractive choice — assumes that a person cannot be a first-time donor in the spring and a repeat donor in the summer — loses because the conclusion is about how many first-time donations arrived, not about whether one person can appear in both quarters. The count can fall or rise either way."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A renowned landscape painter, citing no drainage measurements, says the town should move the river levee two hundred yards west. The council treats the painter's statement as establishing that the levee should be moved.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["treats a celebrated painter's judgment as decisive on a question outside the basis that celebrity supplies","fails to show that the painter has never been mistaken about a public commission","concludes that the present levee caused a flood merely because the painter and the river are mentioned together","assumes that a levee alignment that is safe in one town is required in every town upstream","infers that the whole levee can be moved because one segment of it can be moved"],"a":0,"e":"Where to put a levee is a drainage question. The only support offered is a painter's say-so, with no measurements. Renown as a painter does not establish the engineering conclusion. The attractive choice — fails to show that the painter has never been mistaken about a public commission — loses because a perfect record of past commissions would still not be drainage evidence about this levee. The argument's error is the irrelevant authority, not an unstated claim that the painter is infallible."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"The mill's maintenance book is the only document in which press-roller defects are supposed to be entered. Finding no mention of a warp, the floor manager concludes that the roller has never warped. The book was opened only after the roller had already been in service for three years.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["assumes that a warp is recorded only if the floor manager personally inspects the roller","overlooks that the roller could have warped during the years before any defect was supposed to be written in the book","infers that the manager caused the roller to stay true merely because the book and the roller are mentioned together","takes a property of the press as a whole to be a property of the roller","treats \"warp\" in the manager's conclusion as a different defect from \"warp\" in the maintenance rules"],"a":1,"e":"The conclusion is that the roller never warped. The book, even if complete for its own years, does not cover the first three years of service. A warp in those years would leave the book silent and the conclusion false. The attractive choice — assumes that a warp is recorded only if the floor manager personally inspects the roller — loses because the argument does not depend on which employee writes an entry. The gap is the period the book does not cover, not the identity of the person holding the pen."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The archivist insists that the ink-dating method is trustworthy. Asked why, the archivist says that it matches the dates in the parish register, and that the register's dates are trustworthy because they match the ink-dating method.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["infers that the ink-dating method caused the register's dates to be accurate merely because the two agree","assumes that a dating method is trustworthy only if it was invented in the parish that uses it","supports the method with a register whose trustworthiness is supported only by that same method","treats a method sufficient for dating one letter as necessary for dating every letter in the register","bases the conclusion on registers from a single county and applies it to every county"],"a":2,"e":"Each source is vouched for only by the other. Agreement between them is not an independent ground for either one, so the method's trustworthiness is not established. The attractive choice — infers that the ink-dating method caused the register's dates to be accurate merely because the two agree — loses because agreement is offered as mutual confirmation, not as a cause. The failure is the circle. Reading a causal arrow into the match describes a flaw this argument does not commit."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Hasty generalization","d":2,"s":"The first four tools recovered from the shipwreck's carpenter's locker were cooper's adzes. The expedition report concludes that the locker held only cooper's tools.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism because it","c":["assumes that an adze belongs in the locker only if every tool in the locker is an adze","dismisses the find because the expedition was funded by a museum that collects adzes","treats \"cooper\" in the report as a different trade from \"cooper\" on the tool labels","generalizes about all the tools in the locker from the four that happened to be recovered first","infers that the tools caused the wreck merely because they were found in the locker"],"a":3,"e":"Four cooper's adzes show that the locker held some cooper's tools. The report concludes it held nothing else. The first four tools may be unlike the tools still unrecovered. The attractive choice — assumes that an adze belongs in the locker only if every tool in the locker is an adze — loses because the report does not say the locker may hold an adze only when it holds nothing else. It moves from an initial handful to the locker's tools generally, which is a broader claim than a rule about eligibility."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Post hoc","d":2,"s":"The aviary changed the parrots' feed on the first of the month. Later that month two parrots began plucking feathers. The keeper concludes that the new feed caused the plucking.","q":"The reasoning is flawed because the argument","c":["takes for granted that feather plucking cannot occur unless an aviary changes its feed","assumes that a condition sufficient for a healthy molt is necessary for keeping parrots at all","bases the conclusion on visitors who watched only one of the two birds","treats a feature of each parrot as a feature of the aviary taken as a whole","concludes that the feed change caused the plucking merely because the plucking came after the change"],"a":4,"e":"The only link offered is the order of the two events. Sequence alone does not show that the new feed produced the plucking. The attractive choice — takes for granted that feather plucking cannot occur unless an aviary changes its feed — loses because the keeper claims that this change caused this plucking, not that a feed change is required whenever a parrot plucks. A necessary-condition claim about all aviaries is stronger than the argument and is not its error."},
{"t":"Flaw","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"Scheduling oral arguments in the morning reduced backlog in the county's small-claims court, where each matter takes about fifteen minutes. The administrator concludes that morning arguments will reduce backlog in the felony court, where a single trial can last several days.","q":"The argument is most vulnerable to criticism on the grounds that it","c":["expects a change that sped a docket of very short matters to speed a docket of multi-day trials, without showing that the dockets respond alike","fails to prove that morning arguments are the only reform that could reduce a court's backlog","dismisses the small-claims result because the administrator has a relative who practices in felony court","infers that every judge prefers mornings from the preference of judges who already sit in the morning","treats the word \"backlog\" in the felony conclusion as a different measure from \"backlog\" in the small-claims result"],"a":0,"e":"The small-claims gain is a premise. The conclusion carries it to felony trials whose length is a different constraint. Nothing shows that moving a long trial to the morning frees the kind of time the short matters freed. The attractive choice — fails to prove that morning arguments are the only reform that could reduce a court's backlog — loses because the administrator claims this reform will help the felony court, not that no other reform could reduce backlog. Exclusivity is a stronger thesis than the one argued, and knocking it down misses the analogy."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Neighborhoods that opened tool libraries had lower rates of discarded small appliances the next year than neighborhoods that did not. The sanitation director concludes that opening the libraries caused the lower discard rates.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The neighborhoods that already discarded few appliances were the ones most likely to open tool libraries.","In the neighborhoods that opened tool libraries, nothing other than the libraries accounts for the lower discard rates.","Tool libraries lend more hammers than ladders.","Most neighborhoods in the county have never opened a tool library.","Discarded appliances are photographed before they are hauled away."],"a":1,"e":"If some other difference produced the lower rates, the libraries did not cause them. The causal conclusion needs the denial of those rivals. The attractive choice — The neighborhoods that already discarded few appliances were the ones most likely to open tool libraries. — loses because that story is a rival cause, not a prop for the director. If the low-discard neighborhoods were the ones that opened libraries, the causal claim would be in trouble, which is the opposite of a required assumption."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Of the anglers who returned the wildlife bureau's tagged-trout survey, most favored a shorter season on the reservoir. The bureau concludes that most anglers who fish the reservoir favor a shorter season.","q":"Which one of the following is an assumption required by the argument?","c":["Most anglers who fish the reservoir returned the survey.","A shorter season would raise the number of trout in the reservoir.","On the question of season length, anglers who returned the survey are not substantially unlike reservoir anglers who did not return it.","The bureau has never lengthened the reservoir season.","Tagged trout are easier to catch in cold water than in warm water."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion covers all reservoir anglers. If the people who sent the survey back differed sharply on season length from those who did not, the majority among returners would not carry over. The attractive choice — Most anglers who fish the reservoir returned the survey. — loses because a minority can still represent the reservoir if returners and nonreturners agree on this question. The argument needs that likeness, not a return from most anglers. Negating the trap leaves the conclusion intact."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Custom frames fell from 50 percent of a frame shop's sales to 20 percent. The owner concludes that the shop sold fewer custom frames in the later period.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["Custom frames take longer to build than the shop's ready-made frames.","The shop sells frames only to customers who live in the county.","Ready-made frames were discounted during the later period.","The shop's total number of sales did not rise enough in the later period to offset the smaller share taken by custom frames.","Some of the custom frames are gilded by a second shop across town."],"a":3,"e":"If total sales rose enough, a 20 percent slice could contain more custom frames than the old 50 percent slice. The count conclusion fails unless that offset is ruled out. The attractive choice — Custom frames take longer to build than the shop's ready-made frames. — loses because build time does not connect a smaller share to a smaller count. The conclusion can hold, or fail, whether custom work is slow or fast. Negating the trap does not touch the arithmetic."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"Replacing paper tickets with scannable cards reduced disputes at the municipal stadium's entry gates. The league office concludes that the same replacement will reduce disputes at the fairgrounds arena's entry gates.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The fairgrounds arena does not seat more spectators than the municipal stadium.","Paper tickets at the stadium were printed by the same shop that prints fairgrounds posters.","The league office prefers card scanners made by one manufacturer.","Most spectators at the stadium arrive within twenty minutes of the opening whistle.","Disputes at the arena's gates arise in a way that scannable cards would address, as they did at the stadium."],"a":4,"e":"The stadium result transfers only if the arena's disputes are the kind cards can cut. If the arena's disputes have a different source, the conclusion fails. That likeness is required. The attractive choice — The fairgrounds arena does not seat more spectators than the municipal stadium. — loses because equal seating is not required. A larger arena can still have the same dispute mechanism, and a smaller one can have a different one. Negating the trap, by allowing a larger arena, does not by itself sink the analogy."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"Professor Lang, whose field is medieval Latin paleography, says the town should ban diesel generators at the night market, and offers no further reason. From that remark alone, the council concludes that the ban should be adopted.","q":"Which one of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?","c":["Lang's expertise in medieval Latin paleography qualifies Lang to settle whether the night market should ban diesel generators.","Lang has personally visited the night market.","Diesel generators are louder than the market's acoustic musicians.","No vendor at the market sells fuel.","Paleography manuscripts are sometimes displayed at evening fairs."],"a":0,"e":"The only offered ground is Lang's remark. If paleography does not qualify anyone to settle a generator ban, the council's conclusion does not follow from that remark. The attractive choice — Lang has personally visited the night market. — loses because the argument never uses a site visit. It treats the bare remark as enough. Negating the visit leaves that reliance untouched, so the visit is not a required assumption."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The magazine editor argues: either the profile is cut to 800 words, or the issue will miss its ship date. The profile will not be cut to 800 words. Therefore, the issue will miss its ship date.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["A profile longer than 800 words cannot be printed on the paper the issue already uses.","There is no way for the issue to ship on time while the profile remains longer than 800 words.","The profile's subject lives in the city where the magazine is printed.","The editor has cut other profiles to 800 words in past issues.","Readers prefer profiles that include a full-page portrait."],"a":1,"e":"The disjunctive syllogism reaches a missed ship date only if staying long really leaves no on-time route. If another cut, a dropped photo, or overtime could save the date, the conclusion fails. The attractive choice — A profile longer than 800 words cannot be printed on the paper the issue already uses. — loses because the conclusion is about the ship date, not about whether the paper can hold the words. A long profile can be printable and still miss the date, or printable and still ship. The printing limit is not what the editor needs."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No line in the lighthouse log mentions a failed lamp. The keeper concludes that the lamp has not failed.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The keeper personally writes every line that enters the log.","The lamp is visible from the harbor mouth on clear nights.","If the lamp had failed, a line in the lighthouse log would mention that failure.","The log is stored in a drawer beside the stair.","Lighthouse lamps are replaced on a fixed calendar whether or not they fail."],"a":2,"e":"Silence shows there was no failure only if a failure would have produced a line. Without that link, an unlogged failure is compatible with everything cited. The attractive choice — The keeper personally writes every line that enters the log. — loses because the argument needs a failure to show up in the log, not a particular author. Another keeper's entry would still be a mention. Negating personal authorship does not open a gap the conclusion cannot survive."},
{"t":"Necessary assumption","pattern":"Circular reasoning","d":3,"s":"The choirmaster says the pitch pipe is in tune because it matches the chapel organ, and says the organ is in tune because it matches the pitch pipe. A listener, given only that pair of remarks, concludes that the choirmaster has established that the pitch pipe is in tune.","q":"The argument depends on assuming which one of the following?","c":["The pitch pipe was manufactured more recently than the chapel organ.","The chapel organ is played every Sunday morning.","Most listeners in the chapel can detect a quarter-tone error.","A pair of instruments can establish one instrument's tuning when each is cited only as matching the other.","The choirmaster owns a second pitch pipe at home."],"a":3,"e":"The listener's conclusion is that the circular pair establishes the tuning. That conclusion fails if mutual citation of this kind cannot establish a tuning. The assumption is required by the listener, not by a better argument. The attractive choice — The pitch pipe was manufactured more recently than the chapel organ. — loses because age is unused. A newer pipe can sit in the same circle. Negating the manufacturing claim does not remove the listener's step from the circle to \"established.\""},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"Anyone who completes the field-survey practicum is eligible for the coastal-mapping internship. Nia is eligible for the coastal-mapping internship. Therefore, Nia completed the field-survey practicum.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Most people who are eligible for the coastal-mapping internship completed the field-survey practicum.","The field-survey practicum is offered only in the summer term.","Nia has applied for several internships besides coastal mapping.","Completing the practicum requires a passing mark on the chart quiz.","Only people who have completed the field-survey practicum are eligible for the coastal-mapping internship."],"a":4,"e":"The premise makes the practicum sufficient for eligibility, and Nia is eligible. Adding that eligibility belongs only to people who finished the practicum forces Nia's completion. The conclusion then follows. The attractive choice — Most people who are eligible for the coastal-mapping internship completed the field-survey practicum. — loses because \"most\" leaves room for Nia to be eligible without the practicum. A sufficient assumption has to close that room, and a majority does not."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Each of the six crates in the shipment weighs under 30 pounds. Therefore, the shipment weighs under 30 pounds.","q":"Which one of the following, if assumed, enables the conclusion to be properly drawn?","c":["The shipment weighs no more than its heaviest crate.","Each crate weighs less than the shipment weighs.","The six crates are weighed on the same scale.","Some other shipments include a crate over 30 pounds.","The shipment is insured for more than 30 pounds."],"a":0,"e":"If the shipment is no heavier than its heaviest crate, and every crate is under 30 pounds, the shipment is under 30 pounds. That conditional closes the part-to-whole gap. The attractive choice — Each crate weighs less than the shipment weighs. — loses because each crate being lighter than the whole is the usual arithmetic, and it points the wrong way. It does not cap the shipment at 30 pounds, so the conclusion still does not follow."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Handmade bowls fell from 40 percent of a pottery stall's sales to 25 percent. Therefore, the stall sold fewer handmade bowls in the later period.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["Handmade bowls are priced higher than the stall's molded bowls.","The stall completed the same number of sales in both periods, or fewer sales in the later period.","Most customers at the stall buy a single bowl.","The later period included a holiday market day.","The molded bowls are made in a workshop off the market square."],"a":1,"e":"If the later total is no larger, a drop from 40 percent to 25 percent is a drop in the number of handmade bowls. The count conclusion then follows. The attractive choice — Handmade bowls are priced higher than the stall's molded bowls. — loses because price does not constrain the totals. A smaller share of a much larger later total can still be more handmade bowls, however the bowls are priced."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"A numbered claim check reduced lost coats at the concert-hall cloakroom. Therefore, a numbered claim check will reduce lost coats at the courthouse cloakroom.","q":"Which one of the following, if assumed, enables the conclusion to be properly drawn?","c":["The concert-hall cloakroom handles more coats on a concert night than the courthouse cloakroom handles on a court day.","Courthouse clerks already require visitors to sign a sheet at the door.","Whenever coats are lost because attendants return them without matching a number, a numbered claim check reduces those losses, and that is how coats are lost at the courthouse cloakroom.","The concert hall replaced its claim checks after a single busy weekend.","Most lost coats at the concert hall were wool."],"a":2,"e":"The principle says the check works against losses of that specific kind, and the courthouse losses are of that kind. Together with the concert-hall result used only as background, the courthouse conclusion follows from the principle plus the stated mechanism. The attractive choice — The concert-hall cloakroom handles more coats on a concert night than the courthouse cloakroom handles on a court day. — loses because a difference in volume does not transfer the result, and it does not state a rule that would make the courthouse outcome follow. The conclusion can still fail if the courthouse loses coats for a different reason."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"No line in the orchard's frost log mentions a freeze in the pear block during April. Therefore, the pear block did not freeze in April.","q":"The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?","c":["The frost log is kept in the same shed as the pear block's pruning notes.","Pear trees can survive a light April frost.","The orchard also keeps a log for the apple block.","Every freeze in the pear block during April would have produced a line in the frost log, and the log for April is complete.","April was warmer in a neighboring valley."],"a":3,"e":"If any April freeze would have been entered and the April log is complete, silence entails that there was no freeze. The conclusion follows. The attractive choice — The frost log is kept in the same shed as the pear block's pruning notes. — loses because where the notebook sits does not connect silence to the absence of a freeze. A complete nearby log and an empty nearby log are equally compatible with that detail."},
{"t":"Sufficient assumption","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The backdrop will not be repainted before opening night. Therefore, the opening will be postponed.","q":"Which one of the following, if assumed, enables the conclusion to be properly drawn?","c":["The opening is postponed only if the backdrop is not repainted before opening night.","Some past openings proceeded on time with an old backdrop.","Audiences notice a backdrop that clashes with the costumes.","The scene shop can repaint a flat in a single night.","If the backdrop is not repainted before opening night, the opening will be postponed."],"a":4,"e":"The premise is that the backdrop will not be repainted. The conditional says that this is enough for a postponement. The postponement follows. The attractive choice — The opening is postponed only if the backdrop is not repainted before opening night. — loses because \"only if\" runs the conditional backward: a postponement would imply that the backdrop was not repainted. The company already has the unpainted backdrop and needs the delay to follow, which the reversed link does not give."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"Workshops that replaced solvent-based varnish with a water-based varnish reported fewer headaches among finishers the next month than workshops that kept the solvent varnish. The safety officer concludes that the water-based varnish caused the reduction in headaches.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["Before the change, the two groups of workshops had similar headache rates, similar hours, and similar ventilation, and neither group altered its workload in the month of the reports.","Finishers who already had fewer headaches were more likely to take jobs in the workshops that switched to water-based varnish.","Water-based varnish costs more per gallon than solvent-based varnish.","The safety officer writes a summary of each workshop once a year.","Solvent-based varnish has been sold under the same brand for a decade."],"a":0,"e":"Matching the groups beforehand and holding hours, ventilation, and workload fixed removes the obvious alternative explanations. The varnish change is then better support for the causal claim. The attractive choice — Finishers who already had fewer headaches were more likely to take jobs in the workshops that switched to water-based varnish. — loses because it uses the right two groups and the wrong direction. If the switchers were already the low-headache finishers, the gap is handed to a prior difference and the causal claim is weaker, not stronger."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":3,"s":"Most diners who filled out a comment card on the counter favored dropping the weekday prix-fixe menu. The owner concludes that most of the restaurant's diners favor dropping it.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The comment cards are printed on card stock that does not smear when a plate is set on them.","On the prix-fixe question, diners who fill out the counter card give the same answers, at the same rates, as diners who never touch the card.","The prix-fixe menu changes its soup on Thursdays.","The owner trained in a different city.","Weekday seatings are shorter than Saturday seatings."],"a":1,"e":"The gap is whether card-writers speak for diners who leave no card. Matching answers at matching rates closes that gap and makes the owner's generalization much more likely. The attractive choice — The comment cards are printed on card stock that does not smear when a plate is set on them. — loses because sturdy card stock might keep a form readable beside a plate. It does not show that the people who fill cards out match the people who never do, which is the step the conclusion needs."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"The engineer who wrote the county's levee manual applied the manual's setback rule to new borings of this riverbank and concluded that the levee should move west. The council concludes that the levee should move west.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The engineer is also an amateur watercolorist whose river scenes hang in the council chamber.","A previous levee on this river was built of local clay.","The borings were taken at the depths the manual specifies, and the setback rule is the standard the county uses for this kind of move.","The council meets on the first Tuesday of the month.","West of the present levee the ground is used as a pasture."],"a":2,"e":"The conclusion rests on the engineer's application of a relevant rule. Confirming that the measurements match the manual's method, and that this rule governs the decision, tightens that support. The attractive choice — The engineer is also an amateur watercolorist whose river scenes hang in the council chamber. — loses because painting skill and a picture in the chamber add a kind of renown that is not the manual's rule. The argument is stronger because of the rule and the borings, not because the engineer paints."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The archive's book cradle has supported the twelfth-century psalter at every monthly display for eight years. The conservator concludes that it will support the psalter at this month's display.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["This month's display will add two heavier choir books on the same cradle beside the psalter.","The psalter's binding is goatskin.","Visitors view the display from behind a rope.","This month's display uses the same cradle angle and the same psalter alone, and a check yesterday found the cradle's joints unchanged.","The conservator learned the eight-year record from a single card in the file."],"a":3,"e":"A past success supports the next display when the load and the angle stay within what the cradle has already done and the joints have not changed. Those facts make the conservator's expectation better grounded. The attractive choice — This month's display will add two heavier choir books on the same cradle beside the psalter. — loses because adding heavier books asks the cradle to do something the eight-year record does not cover. That cuts against the conclusion rather than supporting it."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":2,"s":"No entry in the granary's temperature book mentions a bin hotter than the safe limit. The miller concludes that no bin exceeded the limit during the weeks the book covers.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The miller has held the job longer than anyone else now working in the granary.","The bins are numbered in chalk on the alley side.","Grain is delivered on Monday and Thursday.","The temperature book has a cloth cover.","Any bin that exceeded the limit in those weeks would have been entered in the book before the miller read it, and no page from those weeks is missing."],"a":4,"e":"Silence supports \"no bin ran hot\" once a hot bin would have been entered and the pages are all there. Those two facts supply the missing link. The attractive choice — The miller has held the job longer than anyone else now working in the granary. — loses because years on the job do not show that hot bins are recorded. A long-serving miller can still be reading an incomplete book, so the attractive detail leaves the gap open."},
{"t":"Strengthen","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"Sheet-music folios fell from half of a downtown music shop's sales to one fifth. The buyer concludes that the shop sold fewer sheet-music folios in the later month.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?","c":["The shop completed fewer sales in the later month than in the earlier month.","Instrument strings, unlike sheet music, can be purchased without asking a clerk to search the back room.","The buyer once studied violin.","Folios are wrapped in glassine.","The shop's awning was replaced before the later month."],"a":0,"e":"The folio share fell, and the total number of sales also fell. A smaller slice of a smaller total is fewer folios, so the buyer's count is supported. The attractive choice — Instrument strings, unlike sheet music, can be purchased without asking a clerk to search the back room. — loses because how a customer asks for strings may explain a preference. It does not compare the two months' totals, so it leaves the share-to-count step where it was."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":3,"s":"Classrooms that switched from fluorescent tubes to a new broad-spectrum lamp reported fewer afternoon headaches among teachers the next month than classrooms that kept the tubes. The facilities director concludes that the new lamps caused the reduction.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Teacher headache logs are a poor way to rank the overall quality of a classroom renovation.","In that same month, only the classrooms that switched also had their ventilation balanced, and headache rates had matched across the rooms before the month began.","Broad-spectrum lamps cost more per fixture than fluorescent tubes.","The director's memo was typed on a weekday morning.","Fluorescent tubes are sold in boxes of four and boxes of ten."],"a":1,"e":"The ventilation work is a difference present only in the rooms that improved, and the rooms had matched before. That offers an alternative cause for the headache gap and undercuts the lamp explanation. The attractive choice — Teacher headache logs are a poor way to rank the overall quality of a classroom renovation. — loses because the conclusion is about headaches, not about renovation quality in general. A log can be a bad quality index and still record a real drop in headaches. The attractive criticism answers a broader question than the one argued."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"Of the commuters who posted in the transit agency's online forum, most favored eliminating the late-evening bus. The planner concludes that most people who ride that bus favor eliminating it.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["Several posters said they would still buy a monthly pass if the late-evening bus remained.","The forum thread stayed open for four days.","People who post in the forum are far more likely than other riders to want that bus gone, and most riders of the late-evening bus never post.","The late-evening bus uses the same farebox as the daytime buses.","The agency serves a single metro area."],"a":2,"e":"If the people who post are systematically more hostile to the bus than the people who ride it quietly, and most riders never post, the forum majority misrepresents the riders the planner describes. The attractive choice — Several posters said they would still buy a monthly pass if the late-evening bus remained. — loses because willingness to keep a monthly pass is a different attitude from the vote to eliminate the bus. It can be true of posters and still leave their votes typical or atypical. It does not break the generalization."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"A celebrated novelist, citing no seismic readings, says the town should close the cliff path. The parks board concludes that the path should be closed.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The novelist once published an unflattering portrait of the board's chair.","The novelist's house is in a different watershed.","The cliff path is swept of leaves each autumn.","A geotechnical study of this cliff, using the readings the novelist never cited, finds that the path meets the county's stability standard.","The parks board publishes minutes on Thursday."],"a":3,"e":"The conclusion has no seismic support. A study that applies the relevant readings and finds the path meets the governing standard is direct evidence against the closure the novelist urged. The attractive choice — The novelist once published an unflattering portrait of the board's chair. — loses because an unflattering portrait is a personal grievance. It does not bear on whether the cliff path is stable, so it leaves the inference from the novelist's sentence untouched in the way a geotechnical finding does not."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The timber ramp at the boatyard has held the club's launch trailer every May for a decade. The yardmaster concludes that it will hold this May's trailer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The club has used the same model of trailer for the whole decade.","Members prefer to watch launches from the west float.","The yardmaster posts the May date on a chalkboard.","May weather at the yard is often calm.","This May's trailer is far heavier than any trailer the ramp has held, and a split opened in the main timber after last May."],"a":4,"e":"The record concerns lighter trailers on an unsplit ramp. A heavier trailer plus new damage is a reason that record does not reach this May, so the yardmaster's conclusion is undermined. The attractive choice — The club has used the same model of trailer for the whole decade. — loses because using one model for the whole decade, if anything, keeps the past cases comparable. It does not show that this year's load or the timber has changed, so it does not weaken the projection."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The printer argues: either the color section is dropped, or the Saturday edition will miss the trucks. The color section will not be dropped. Therefore, the Saturday edition will miss the trucks.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["A second press, already warm, has taken the color section on tighter schedules than tonight's and is assigned to do so again.","The trucks have enough cargo space for a Saturday edition that includes the color section.","The color section was designed on Wednesday.","Subscribers are told that Saturday includes a color section.","The printer has dropped color sections before other deadlines."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion needs keeping the color section to miss the trucks. A second press that can run that section and still make the trucks breaks that horn of the either-or. The attractive choice — The trucks have enough cargo space for a Saturday edition that includes the color section. — loses because cargo space would matter if the paper were ready and too bulky to load. The argument's risky step is whether the edition can be finished on time with the color section kept. Room on the truck does not show that it can."},
{"t":"Weaken","pattern":"Ad hominem","d":3,"s":"Because the nutritionist's cousin owns a rival wholesale house, a hospital trustee dismisses the nutritionist's spoilage counts and concludes that the counts do not warrant dropping the current produce vendor.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most weakens the argument?","c":["The nutritionist has, on two earlier occasions, urged the hospital to buy goods the cousin sells.","An inspector with no wholesale business, using the same spoilage counts, found that spoilage rose only after the current vendor was hired and recommended the same change.","The current vendor began deliveries on a Monday.","Spoilage counts are entered by the kitchen supervisor.","The trustee is a retired pharmacist."],"a":1,"e":"The trustee's only ground for discarding the counts is the cousin's business. An uninvolved inspector reading those counts the same way shows that the recommendation does not depend on that interest, which undercuts the dismissal. The attractive choice — The nutritionist has, on two earlier occasions, urged the hospital to buy goods the cousin sells. — loses because earlier recommendations deepen the family-interest story the trustee already uses. They support setting the nutritionist aside. They do not show that the counts themselves fail to warrant dropping the vendor."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":3,"s":"If a mosaic is accessioned, it is photographed. If it is photographed, a condition note is filed. Mosaic 18 has been accessioned. Mosaic 19 has not been photographed.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["A condition note has been filed for mosaic 19.","Mosaic 18 has not been photographed.","A condition note has been filed for mosaic 18.","Mosaic 19 was accessioned.","Every mosaic with a condition note on file has been accessioned."],"a":2,"e":"Accession brings a photograph, and a photograph brings a condition note. Mosaic 18 was accessioned, so both steps apply to it and a note was filed. The attractive choice — A condition note has been filed for mosaic 19. — loses because mosaic 19 was not photographed, so the second conditional never starts for it. Nothing in the statements puts a condition note on 19. The numeral is easy to swap with 18 on a quick read."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"A bindery completed the same number of jobs in the spring quarter and the summer quarter. Leather jobs fell from 40 percent of those jobs to 25 percent.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The bindery completed fewer jobs of every kind in the summer quarter than in the spring quarter.","Leather jobs take more hours than cloth jobs at this bindery.","The bindery will complete fewer leather jobs next quarter.","The bindery completed fewer leather jobs in the summer quarter than in the spring quarter.","No cloth job was completed in the spring quarter."],"a":3,"e":"The total is unchanged and leather's share fell, so the leather count fell. That comparison is fixed by the two statements. The attractive choice — The bindery completed fewer jobs of every kind in the summer quarter than in the spring quarter. — loses because only leather's share is said to fall. With the total held constant, some other kind of job must have taken a larger share and may have grown. \"Every kind\" is stronger than the arithmetic."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":3,"s":"A survey permit is issued only if the applicant has posted a bond. Silva has not posted a bond.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Silva will be issued a survey permit if Silva posts a bond.","Every applicant who posts a bond is issued a survey permit.","Silva's application was filed on a Monday.","Bonds for survey permits cost more this year than last year.","Silva has not been issued a survey permit."],"a":4,"e":"\"Only if\" makes the bond necessary for a permit. No bond, therefore no permit. Silva is ruled out. The attractive choice — Silva will be issued a survey permit if Silva posts a bond. — loses because posting a bond is not said to be enough. The rule can require a bond and still withhold a permit for a second reason. The attractive conditional turns a necessary condition into a sufficient one."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":2,"s":"No one who missed the calibration seminar may sign the survey plates. Otis signed a survey plate this morning. Pilar missed the calibration seminar.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Pilar may not sign the survey plates.","Otis attended every seminar the office offered this year.","Pilar signed a survey plate this morning.","Otis missed the calibration seminar.","The plates Otis signed had been engraved before the seminar was held."],"a":0,"e":"Missing the seminar bars a person from signing. Pilar missed it, so Pilar may not sign. That is the rule applied to Pilar. The attractive choice — Otis attended every seminar the office offered this year. — loses because Otis signed, so Otis did not miss this seminar. The statements say nothing about the other seminars on the office calendar. \"Every seminar\" adds a schedule the rule never mentions."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Part-to-whole","d":2,"s":"Every crew that goes into the field is assigned its own van, and no van carries more than one crew. The bureau has 40 vans. Fifty crews are on the roster.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["At least 10 of the rostered crews are unqualified to go into the field.","At most 40 of the rostered crews can be in the field at the same time.","The bureau will buy ten more vans before the next season.","Some vans carry two crews when the roster is fully assigned.","Every rostered crew was in the field yesterday."],"a":1,"e":"Each crew in the field uses a distinct van, and there are 40 vans. No arrangement puts more than 40 crews out together. The roster's extra ten have to wait, not fail a test. The attractive choice — At least 10 of the rostered crews are unqualified to go into the field. — loses because the extra crews may be fully qualified and simply unable to go out together with the others. A cap on simultaneous trips is not a finding about competence."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional reversal/negation","d":3,"s":"If the studio kiln is vented during the firing, the glaze will not blister. This firing's glaze blistered.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The glaze blistered because the kiln was left unvented.","The studio kiln is never vented.","The studio kiln was not vented during this firing.","Every blistered glaze in the city comes from this studio.","Venting a kiln costs more than leaving it closed."],"a":2,"e":"Venting is sufficient for a blister-free glaze. The glaze blistered, so that sufficient condition was absent. The kiln was not vented. The attractive choice — The glaze blistered because the kiln was left unvented. — loses because \"because\" names the unvented kiln as the cause. The conditional only says that venting would have prevented blistering. Something else could have blistered a glaze that was also unvented. Absence of the safeguard is required; the causal story is not."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Quantifier scope","d":3,"s":"Most of the jurors who have served on a patent trial this year have also served on a contract trial. Several jurors who served on a patent trial this year have never served on a contract trial.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Most jurors who served on a contract trial this year also served on a patent trial.","Every juror who served on a patent trial this year served on a contract trial.","No juror served on both a patent trial and a contract trial this year.","At least one juror who served on a patent trial this year also served on a contract trial.","Several jurors who served only on contract trials have never served on a patent trial."],"a":3,"e":"\"Most\" of a group includes at least one. So at least one patent-trial juror has also served on a contract trial. The second sentence is compatible with that, since \"most\" leaves room for several exceptions. The attractive choice — Most jurors who served on a contract trial this year also served on a patent trial. — loses because the overlap is stated from the patent side. It does not say what share of the contract jurors came from patent trials. Reversing \"most\" is the natural misread, and it does not follow."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Conditional chain","d":2,"s":"Anyone whose badge is red has passed the tank inspection. Anyone who has passed the tank inspection may enter the dry dock. Quinn's badge is red. Rhee has not passed the tank inspection.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Rhee may not enter the dry dock.","Rhee may enter the dry dock.","Quinn has not passed the tank inspection.","Everyone who may enter the dry dock has a red badge.","Quinn may enter the dry dock."],"a":4,"e":"A red badge means the inspection was passed, and a passed inspection permits entry. Quinn's badge is red, so Quinn may enter. The attractive choice — Rhee may not enter the dry dock. — loses because failing the inspection blocks a red badge. It does not, under these rules, block entry. Entry is granted to anyone who passed, and the rules never say that only those people may enter. The attractive ban on Rhee reverses a sufficient condition."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"The choir sold the same number of tickets to the two concerts. Student tickets were a smaller share of the tickets at the second concert than at the first.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Fewer student tickets were sold for the second concert than for the first.","Fewer people attended the second concert than the first.","Student tickets cost less than general tickets.","No student attended the first concert.","The second concert was longer than the first."],"a":0,"e":"Equal totals and a smaller student share fix a smaller student count at the second concert. The attractive choice — Fewer people attended the second concert than the first. — loses because attendance is not the same thing as tickets sold, and the statements set the ticket totals equal. A smaller student share does not shrink the house."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Sufficient/necessary confusion","d":2,"s":"The lab releases a dye batch only if that batch has cleared the residue assay. Dye batch K was released this morning.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["Dye batch K cleared every assay the lab runs.","Dye batch K cleared the residue assay.","Dye batch K was released because the residue assay is the lab's cheapest test.","Every dye batch that clears the residue assay is released the same morning.","The lab released no dye batch other than K this morning."],"a":1,"e":"Release happens only if the residue assay has been cleared. Batch K was released, so batch K cleared that assay. The attractive choice — Dye batch K cleared every assay the lab runs. — loses because the rule names one assay. Clearing it is required, and no other assay is mentioned. \"Every assay\" adds screens the release rule does not contain."},
{"t":"Must be true","pattern":"Whole-to-part","d":2,"s":"All of the copper panels now on the roof were annealed before installation. The south slope is covered entirely by those copper panels.","q":"If the statements above are true, which one of the following must be true?","c":["The south slope was annealed before any other slope on the building.","The north slope is covered with copper panels.","Every panel on the south slope was annealed before installation.","Annealing makes a copper panel heavier.","No annealed panel was installed on the south slope."],"a":2,"e":"The south slope's cover is a subset of the annealed copper panels. Every panel in a subset of annealed panels was annealed. The attractive choice — The south slope was annealed before any other slope on the building. — loses because nothing orders the slopes in time. The south slope's panels were annealed, and another slope's panels may have been annealed earlier. The attractive comparison adds a race the statements do not run."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Correlation vs. causation","d":2,"s":"In four separate summers, playgrounds that installed shade sails had fewer heat-related nurse visits than those same playgrounds had the summer before, and the logs record no change in hours, staffing, or the age mix of the children.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The shade sails were the sole cause of every heat-related visit that those playgrounds avoided.","Playgrounds without shade sails had more nurse visits than playgrounds with sails in every city that keeps such logs.","Shade sails will be installed on every playground in the district next summer.","The shade sails are a credible candidate for having contributed to the drop in heat-related visits at those playgrounds.","Children who visit a shaded playground never suffer a heat-related illness."],"a":3,"e":"The same sites improved after the sails went in, four times, with the obvious operational changes not showing up in the logs. That pattern supports a cautious causal candidate. It does not pin every avoided visit on the sails alone. The attractive choice — The shade sails were the sole cause of every heat-related visit that those playgrounds avoided. — loses because \"sole cause\" and \"every\" visit close doors the logs never close. Unlogged weather differences, for example, remain possible. The strong wording outruns a pattern that only makes the sails plausible."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Absence of evidence","d":3,"s":"The darkroom's chemical log is the only place a spoiled developer batch is recorded. Every spoiled batch this year would have been recorded in it. The conservator has the complete log, and it names no spoiled batch.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The darkroom will not spoil a developer batch next year.","The conservator personally mixes every developer batch.","Developer is the only chemical the darkroom uses.","A spoiled batch is more expensive than a usable one.","No developer batch spoiled in that darkroom this year."],"a":4,"e":"A spoiled batch would have been entered, the log is complete, and the log is empty of spoiled batches. The supported conclusion is that none spoiled this year. The attractive choice — The darkroom will not spoil a developer batch next year. — loses because the log speaks to this year. Next year is a fresh period, and nothing says the recording practice or the chemistry will stay as it is. The future claim is a step past the evidence."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Unrepresentative sample","d":2,"s":"The only families asked about the Saturday story hour were families who already attend it, and most of them asked for a second hour. Families who do not attend were not asked.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The replies do not establish that most families in the library's district want a second Saturday story hour.","Most families in the library's district oppose a second Saturday story hour.","The story hour should be cancelled.","Every family that attends the story hour has a library card.","Saturday is the only day the children's room is open."],"a":0,"e":"The sample is drawn only from people who already come. Their wish for a second hour does not establish the district-wide majority the sentence denies. That negative conclusion is what the narrow sample supports. The attractive choice — Most families in the library's district oppose a second Saturday story hour. — loses because silence from non-attenders does not show that they oppose the hour. They might favor it, split, or have no view. Opposition is the flashy reverse of the attendees' wish, and it is not supported."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Percentages vs. numbers","d":2,"s":"A print shop completed fewer jobs this March than last March. Rush jobs were a larger share of the jobs this March than last March.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The shop completed fewer rush jobs this March than last March.","The figures do not establish that the shop completed more rush jobs this March than last March.","Rush jobs are less profitable than ordinary jobs at this shop.","The shop will complete fewer jobs next March.","No ordinary job was completed this March."],"a":1,"e":"A larger share of a smaller total can be more rush jobs, fewer, or the same number. The two facts therefore do not establish an increase. That is the supported conclusion. The attractive choice — The shop completed fewer rush jobs this March than last March. — loses because a smaller total does not drag every subcategory down when its share rises. \"Fewer rush jobs\" is one possibility, not the one the figures pick out. It looks like the arithmetic and it is not forced."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Weak analogy","d":3,"s":"Lost tools at the scene shop fell after a shadow-board was installed. Tools at the costume shop are lost for a different reason: they leave in unlogged loan bags, and the costume shop has no wall where a shadow-board would show a missing tool.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["A shadow-board would increase losses at the costume shop.","The costume shop should stop lending tools.","The scene shop's result is a weak reason to expect a shadow-board to cut losses at the costume shop.","Every tool in the scene shop is heavier than every tool in the costume shop.","Unlogged loan bags are used at the scene shop as well."],"a":2,"e":"The mechanism that the board fixed at the scene shop is not the mechanism at the costume shop, and the costume shop lacks the wall the board needs. The transfer is poorly supported. That is as far as the facts go. The attractive choice — A shadow-board would increase losses at the costume shop. — loses because a weak analogy fails to support a benefit. It does not support the opposite prediction that losses would rise. The attractive forecast invents an effect the facts do not describe."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Appeal to authority","d":2,"s":"The only recommendation to close the footpath is a sonnet published by a poet who cites no trail inspection. The county's trail standard, which the poet does not discuss, is the rule the board must apply.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The trail standard requires the board to keep the footpath open.","The poet has walked the footpath.","Sonnets are a poor way to memorize trail rules.","The sonnet does not establish that the board should close the footpath.","The board has closed other footpaths this year."],"a":3,"e":"A poem with no inspection is the only recommendation, and the decision is governed by a standard the poet never applies. That is enough to say the sonnet does not establish the closure. It is not a reading of what the standard requires. The attractive choice — The trail standard requires the board to keep the footpath open. — loses because the standard is identified as the rule, and its content is not given. It might require closure once an inspection is done. \"Keep the path open\" adds a verdict the statements do not contain."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"Past-to-future","d":3,"s":"The ferry's backup generator started on the first try in each of the last thirty weekly tests, always with the same fuel and the same technician. Tomorrow's test is scheduled with that fuel and that technician, and the generator has not been altered since the last test.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The backup generator cannot fail to start in tomorrow's test.","The generator was installed thirty weeks ago.","The ferry will not need the backup generator tomorrow.","The technician is the only person certified to buy the fuel.","There is good reason to expect the backup generator to start on the first try in tomorrow's test."],"a":4,"e":"Thirty matched successes and an unaltered machine, with tomorrow's conditions held to the same fuel and technician, support a reasonable expectation. They do not make a failure impossible. The attractive choice — The backup generator cannot fail to start in tomorrow's test. — loses because \"cannot fail\" converts a strong pattern into a guarantee. A matched record raises the probability. It does not close every mechanical surprise, so the absolute claim is not what the facts support."},
{"t":"Most strongly supported","pattern":"False dichotomy","d":2,"s":"The company will not cancel the outdoor screening. It will postpone the screening by one hour if rain is falling at the advertised time, and that postponement has kept every previous screening intact. Rain is the only threat on tonight's forecast.","q":"Which one of the following is most strongly supported by the information above?","c":["The forecast does not show that tonight's screening will be lost.","Tonight's screening will be held at the advertised time.","The company has never screened a film indoors.","Rain is forecast for the entire weekend.","Audiences prefer a postponed screening to a cancelled one."],"a":0,"e":"Cancellation is off the table, and the only forecast threat is a kind of rain the company already rides out by waiting an hour. The facts support that the screening is not shown to be lost. They do not fix the clock. The attractive choice — Tonight's screening will be held at the advertised time. — loses because if rain is falling at the advertised time, the company postpones. The screening may start an hour late. \"At the advertised time\" ignores the postponement the stimulus itself describes."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Only ushers who worked opening night were asked, and most of them want a second intermission. Opening-night ushers are not a stand-in for the whole house crew. So the company has not shown that most of the house crew want a second intermission.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Opening-night ushers are not a stand-in for the whole house crew.","The company has not shown that most of the house crew want a second intermission.","Most ushers who worked opening night want a second intermission.","The company should add a second intermission.","Ushers who missed opening night were asked a different question."],"a":1,"e":"The narrow sample and the warning about stand-ins are offered to support a verdict about what the company has shown. That verdict is the conclusion. The attractive choice — Opening-night ushers are not a stand-in for the whole house crew. — loses because the stand-in sentence is the rule the argument uses. It would still be doing its work if the verdict were left unstated. It is support, and it is the sentence a quick read is most likely to underline."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"A passed sterility screen is required for release and is not enough to force one. Batch C passed the screen, and a supervisor concludes that batch C has therefore been released. That conclusion does not follow.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["A passed sterility screen is required for release and is not enough to force one.","Batch C failed the sterility screen.","The supervisor's conclusion that batch C has been released does not follow.","The supervisor should be removed from the release desk.","No batch that passed the screen has ever been held back."],"a":2,"e":"The screen rule is cited so that the supervisor's inference can be rejected. The argument's point is that the inference does not follow. The attractive choice — A passed sterility screen is required for release and is not enough to force one. — loses because the screen rule is the reason. It is true in the argument and it is not what the argument is trying to get the reader to accept about the supervisor. The attractive sentence is the premise that does the work."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"The only brief urging a new flood siren is a letter from a portrait painter who measured no distances. The fire code, which the letter never applies, is the rule the council must use. A letter that never applies that code is not a reason to buy the siren. The letter therefore does not give the council a reason to buy it.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The council must use the fire code.","The painter measured the distances before writing the letter.","The fire code requires the council to reject the siren.","The letter does not give the council a reason to buy the flood siren.","The council should ignore every public comment about sirens."],"a":3,"e":"The code and the missing measurements are there to support a judgment about the letter. The judgment is that the letter is not a reason to buy the siren. The attractive choice — The council must use the fire code. — loses because the duty to use the code is a premise. The argument does not stop at reminding the council of its rule. It uses the rule to set the letter aside."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":3,"s":"The generator started on the first try in thirty matched tests, and tomorrow's test matches them. A matched record supports an expectation. It does not make a failure impossible. The duty officer's claim that the generator cannot fail tomorrow is therefore stronger than the record.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["A matched record supports an expectation and does not make a failure impossible.","The generator failed its most recent test.","Tomorrow's test will use a different fuel.","The duty officer should cancel tomorrow's sailing.","The duty officer's claim that the generator cannot fail tomorrow is stronger than the record supports."],"a":4,"e":"The general point about matched records is applied to the duty officer. The sentence the argument is driving at is that this officer's \"cannot fail\" overreaches the thirty tests. The attractive choice — A matched record supports an expectation and does not make a failure impossible. — loses because the general point is the rule the argument applies. It is not the verdict about the duty officer, which is where the argument comes to rest."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Leather's share of the bindery's jobs fell, and the bindery completed the same number of jobs in both quarters. From the share alone, a clerk concludes that every kind of job declined. The clerk's conclusion ignores the fixed total.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["The clerk's conclusion that every kind of job declined ignores the fixed total.","Leather's share of the bindery's jobs fell while the number of jobs stayed the same.","The bindery completed fewer jobs in the summer.","The clerk should recount the leather jobs by hand.","Cloth jobs are the only jobs the bindery does."],"a":0,"e":"The share and the total are the facts. The argument uses them to criticize the clerk. That criticism is the main conclusion. The attractive choice — Leather's share of the bindery's jobs fell while the number of jobs stayed the same. — loses because the share-and-total sentence is the evidence. It can be true and still not be the claim the argument is making about the clerk."},
{"t":"Main point","pattern":"Main conclusion","d":2,"s":"Packages leave the mailroom because they sit on an open table, and a locked cage stopped that loss. The loading dock loses packages through an unlatched gate, and it has no open table. So the mailroom result is a poor reason to expect a locked cage to stop the dock's losses.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the argument?","c":["Packages leave the mailroom because they sit on an open table.","The mailroom result is a poor reason to expect a locked cage to stop losses at the loading dock.","The loading dock should install a locked cage this week.","The mailroom loses packages through an unlatched gate.","A locked cage would raise losses at the loading dock."],"a":1,"e":"The open table and the dock's different leak explain why the mailroom result does not travel. The conclusion is the verdict on that result as a reason. The attractive choice — Packages leave the mailroom because they sit on an open table. — loses because the open-table sentence is a premise about how the mailroom loses packages. The argument cites it on the way to a conclusion about the analogy."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Either the mural is moved to the interior stair, or the winter damp will destroy it. The mural will not be moved to the interior stair. Therefore, the winter damp will destroy it.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that no third course could save the mural from the winter damp","citing a conservator's measurements of the winter damp","reaching a result by ruling out one of two alternatives that the argument treats as exhaustive","inferring a cause from the fact that damp and damage have occurred together","generalizing from one mural to every mural in the building"],"a":2,"e":"The argument states an either-or, rejects the move, and takes the remaining side. That is elimination between two stated options. It does not demonstrate that the list was complete. The attractive choice — showing that no third course could save the mural from the winter damp — loses because completeness is assumed in the either-or, not shown. The attractive description credits the argument with a proof it never attempts. The method is the elimination, not a survey of other courses."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"The glaze blistered, and the firing was unvented. The studio head concludes that leaving the kiln unvented caused the blistering, since the blistering was noticed after the vent was left shut.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning?","c":["applying a general rule that an unvented kiln always blisters a glaze","rejecting a causal claim because of the head's financial interest in the kiln","inferring that a property of the glaze is a property of the kiln","treating the order of two events as sufficient to show that the earlier event caused the later one","concluding that the vent was shut because the glaze blistered"],"a":3,"e":"The only support named is that the blistering was noticed after the vent was left shut. The head reads that order as causation. No general \"always\" rule is stated or applied. The attractive choice — applying a general rule that an unvented kiln always blisters a glaze — loses because a universal rule about unvented kilns is not in the argument. The head relies on sequence in this firing. The attractive description imports a principle the head did not use."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Most guests who filled out a card at the hotel's front desk said breakfast should start later. The manager concludes that most guests in the hotel want a later breakfast.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that guests who never stopped at the desk were polled and disagreed","defining \"guest\" as a day visitor in the premise and as an overnight guest in the conclusion","concluding that a later breakfast caused higher occupancy because the two moved together","rejecting the card-writers' view because of the manager's own schedule","taking the majority view of guests who chose to fill out a card to be the majority view of the hotel's guests"],"a":4,"e":"The premise is a majority among guests who filled out cards. The conclusion is a majority of the hotel's guests. The method is that transfer, with no further sample described. The attractive choice — showing that guests who never stopped at the desk were polled and disagreed — loses because guests who skipped the desk are not said to have been polled at all. The attractive description adds a conflicting poll the manager does not use. The method is the unsupported expansion, not a comparison of two polls."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":3,"s":"Custom orders were 40 percent of the letterpress jobs and are now 15 percent. The scheduler concludes that the shop now prints fewer custom orders, without comparing the number of jobs in the two periods.","q":"Which one of the following most accurately describes the method of reasoning?","c":["inferring a change in a count from a change in a percentage, with no information about the relevant totals","establishing that the shop's total number of jobs fell between the two periods","appealing to a printer's authority about which jobs are custom","treating a feature of each custom order as a feature of the shop's whole output","rejecting the percentage because the scheduler dislikes custom work"],"a":0,"e":"The only figure given is the custom share. The scheduler reads it as a count. The method is that percentage-to-number step, and the totals are explicitly unused. The attractive choice — establishing that the shop's total number of jobs fell between the two periods — loses because the totals are exactly what the argument leaves out. The attractive description says the argument established a fact it never had. That is why the count does not follow, and it is not the method used."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"A novelist who cites no readings says the cliff path should close. A commissioner treats that sentence as settling the question.","q":"The commissioner's argument proceeds by","c":["showing that the novelist has inspected the cliff and applied the county's stability standard","treating a novelist's unsupported assertion as decisive on a question the assertion does not address with evidence","inferring that the path eroded because closure and erosion were discussed together","generalizing from one cliff path to every path in the county","dismissing the novelist because of a personal disagreement"],"a":1,"e":"The commissioner's entire move is to adopt the novelist's sentence. No inspection is supplied. The method is reliance on that bare assertion. The attractive choice — showing that the novelist has inspected the cliff and applied the county's stability standard — loses because an inspection under the standard is what the argument lacks. The attractive description awards the commissioner a method that would have been relevant and that the stimulus withholds."},
{"t":"Method","pattern":"Argument method","d":2,"s":"Each of the spare sails weighs little enough for one rigger to carry. The bo'sun concludes that the bundle of spare sails weighs little enough for one rigger to carry.","q":"The argument proceeds by","c":["showing that the bundle weighs no more than the heaviest sail in it","appealing to the bo'sun's authority about sailcloth","attributing to a bundle a limit that has been established only for each sail in the bundle","inferring a cause from the order in which the sails were bundled","using \"carry\" in the premise for a legal duty and in the conclusion for a physical task"],"a":2,"e":"The premise is about each sail. The conclusion is about the bundle. The method is that transfer, and nothing is said that would cap the bundle at the heaviest sail. The attractive choice — showing that the bundle weighs no more than the heaviest sail in it — loses because a cap at the heaviest sail would have made the conclusion safe, and the argument does not provide it. The attractive description states a missing premise as if it were the method already used."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**Replies from shoppers who joined the loyalty program do not represent the store's shoppers.** Only loyalty members were asked, and most of them want the store to drop paper bags. So the store has not shown that most of its shoppers want paper bags dropped.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is the main conclusion of the argument.","It is an intermediate conclusion used to support a recommendation to keep paper bags.","It is a hypothesis the argument rejects.","It is a premise offered in support of the conclusion that the store has not shown what most shoppers want.","It is background about bag prices that the reasoning does not rely on."],"a":3,"e":"The final sentence is the conclusion, and it is about what the store has shown. The boldfaced sentence gives the sampling rule that supports that conclusion. The attractive choice — It is the main conclusion of the argument. — loses because the boldfaced sentence is general and prominent, so it looks like the point. The argument uses it to reach a further verdict about this poll. A premise that could have been a conclusion elsewhere is still a premise here."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"A countersignature is necessary before a plat can be filed, and it is not sufficient. This plat bears a countersignature. **The claim that this plat has been filed does not follow.**","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise used to show that a countersignature is not sufficient for filing.","It is an objection the argument mentions in order to set it aside.","It is a definition of the word \"countersignature.\"","It is an example of a plat that lacked a countersignature.","It is the conclusion of the argument."],"a":4,"e":"The rule and the countersignature are given so that the boldfaced verdict can be drawn. Nothing is inferred from that verdict. It is the conclusion. The attractive choice — It is a premise used to show that a countersignature is not sufficient for filing. — loses because the insufficiency of the countersignature is already stated before the boldfaced sentence and is the reason for it. The attractive role runs the support backward, turning the conclusion into a premise for a point already granted."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"**The choir will not move rehearsal to the morning.** The director says that either rehearsal moves to the morning or the evening slot will be lost to the play, and concludes that the evening slot will be lost.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise that, together with the either-or claim, is offered as yielding the conclusion.","It is the conclusion that the evening slot will be lost to the play.","It is an alternative the director rejects as impossible in every season.","It is evidence that the evening slot has already been lost.","It is a recommendation the argument refuses to consider."],"a":0,"e":"The conclusion is that the evening slot will be lost. The boldfaced sentence denies the other alternative. It is one of the two premises in the elimination. The attractive choice — It is the conclusion that the evening slot will be lost to the play. — loses because the loss of the evening slot is what the director is trying to establish. The boldfaced sentence states a decision already taken, not that result. The attractive role assigns the conclusion's content to the wrong clause."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":3,"s":"The payroll clerk notes that overtime fell from one third of paid hours to one tenth. **The shop paid fewer overtime hours.** No total for either month is given.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise stating the shop's total paid hours in the later month.","It is the conclusion the clerk draws from the change in share.","It is an objection a critic raises against the clerk.","It is a definition of \"overtime.\"","It is an intermediate figure from which the share is calculated."],"a":1,"e":"The share change is the noted fact. The boldfaced sentence is what the clerk concludes from it. The last sentence points out that no total was supplied for that inference. The attractive choice — It is a premise stating the shop's total paid hours in the later month. — loses because a total is precisely what the argument says is missing. The boldfaced sentence states a count of overtime hours, not a total of all paid hours, and it is inferred rather than given. The attractive role both mislabels the sentence and invents a figure."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"Each coil of line is light enough for one hand. **The crate of coils is light enough for one hand.** That is what the rigger concludes from the weight of the separate coils.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise about the weight of each individual coil.","It is a finding the rigger cites in order to abandon it.","It is the conclusion of the argument.","It is a definition of the word \"crate.\"","It is evidence that one of the coils is heavier than the limit."],"a":2,"e":"The stimulus says the boldfaced sentence is the conclusion drawn from the separate coils. Each coil's weight is the premise. The crate is the result. The attractive choice — It is a premise about the weight of each individual coil. — loses because the per-coil fact is the preceding sentence. The attractive role moves that premise into the boldfaced clause, which talks about the crate rather than about each coil."},
{"t":"Role","pattern":"Statement role","d":2,"s":"Twelve matched batches of the glaze fired clean. **The next batch cannot craze.** The potter draws that conclusion from the twelve batches alone.","q":"The boldfaced statement plays which one of the following roles in the argument?","c":["It is a premise describing the result of the twelve batches.","It is an objection the argument raises against the potter.","It is a definition of a matched batch.","It is the conclusion the potter draws from the matched batches.","It is background the potter does not use."],"a":3,"e":"The stimulus says the potter draws the boldfaced sentence from the batches. The batches are the support. The \"cannot craze\" sentence is the conclusion. The attractive choice — It is a premise describing the result of the twelve batches. — loses because the twelve batches are the opening sentence, not the boldfaced one. The attractive role describes those results as if they were the sentence that actually predicts the next batch."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: A city may fell a street tree only if the tree is dead or the tree is damaging a foundation. The elm on Canal Street is alive, and it is not damaging a foundation.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The city must fell the elm on Canal Street if the elm later damages a foundation.","The city must fell every dead street tree before it inspects foundations.","The principle permits felling the elm because Canal Street has other trees.","A tree that is alive may be felled whenever a neighbor requests it.","The principle does not permit the city to fell the elm on Canal Street."],"a":4,"e":"\"Only if\" bars felling unless one of the two conditions holds. Neither holds for this elm, so felling it is not permitted. That is the judgment the principle and the facts deliver. The attractive choice — The city must fell the elm on Canal Street if the elm later damages a foundation. — loses because \"only if\" does not create a duty to fell once a condition appears. Even a later foundation problem would not, under this principle, require the city to fell the tree. The attractive judgment turns a limit into an order."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: If a survey's respondents are drawn only from people who already use a service, the survey does not establish what most residents think of that service. The tram poll questioned only people who rode the tram last week.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The tram poll does not establish what most residents think of the tram.","Most residents oppose the tram.","The tram poll establishes that most riders want more frequent service.","No resident who avoids the tram may be asked about it.","The city must expand tram service."],"a":0,"e":"The respondents are current users. The principle says that kind of survey does not establish the residents' view. The conforming judgment stops at that failure of proof. The attractive choice — Most residents oppose the tram. — loses because failure to establish a favorable or any majority is not evidence of opposition. The attractive sentence supplies a verdict the principle never reaches."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: If a category's share of a fixed total falls, the number of items in that category falls. Commissioned scores fell from 30 percent of the orchestra's programs to 10 percent, and the orchestra played the same number of programs in both seasons.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The orchestra played fewer works of every kind in the later season.","The orchestra played fewer commissioned scores in the later season.","The orchestra should commission no scores next season.","Commissioned scores are harder to rehearse than repertoire scores.","The later season's programs were longer."],"a":1,"e":"The total of programs is fixed and the commissioned share fell. The principle then says the commissioned count fell. The attractive choice — The orchestra played fewer works of every kind in the later season. — loses because the principle moves one share to one count. Other kinds of work can grow inside a fixed total. \"Every kind\" is a wider verdict than the principle allows."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: A run of past successes supports expecting the next attempt only when the next attempt matches the conditions of those successes. The backup pump started in twenty tests on diesel. The next test will use a different fuel.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The pump will fail to start on the different fuel.","The twenty diesel starts prove the pump will start on any fuel.","The twenty diesel starts do not support expecting the pump to start on the different fuel.","The pump should be tested only on diesel.","A different fuel is cheaper than diesel."],"a":2,"e":"The fuel does not match, so the principle withholds support for the expectation. The conforming judgment is that the record does not support the expectation, not a prediction of failure. The attractive choice — The pump will fail to start on the different fuel. — loses because withholding support is not a forecast that the attempt fails. The pump might start on the new fuel for a new reason. The attractive sentence is stronger than the principle."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: If an authority's only stated ground is expertise in an unrelated field, that statement does not settle a technical question. A celebrated harpist, citing no soil tests, says the retaining wall should be rebuilt, and no other ground is offered.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The retaining wall should be left as it is.","The harpist's recitals draw large audiences.","Soil tests are more expensive than harp strings.","The harpist's statement does not settle whether the retaining wall should be rebuilt.","Every retaining wall in the city should be rebuilt."],"a":3,"e":"The only ground is harp expertise, which is unrelated to a wall. The principle says the statement does not settle the technical question. That is the judgment. The attractive choice — The retaining wall should be left as it is. — loses because an unsettled question is not a verdict for the status quo. The wall might still need rebuilding on other evidence. The attractive sentence decides the issue the principle leaves open."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: An empty incident slate shows that a kind of event did not happen in a period only when any such event would have been chalked on that slate and the slate for the period is intact. The boathouse slate for August is intact, any collision in August would have been chalked on it, and the slate shows no collision.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["No collision will occur at the boathouse in September.","Collisions are chalked only when the dockmaster is present.","The boathouse is the oldest shed on the water.","August was windier than July.","No collision occurred at the boathouse in August."],"a":4,"e":"Both conditions are met for August, and the slate is empty of collisions. The principle therefore yields that no collision occurred at the boathouse in August. The attractive choice — No collision will occur at the boathouse in September. — loses because the principle, as applied, covers the intact August slate. September is another month, and nothing says a September slate exists. The attractive forecast leaves the period the slate actually covers."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: A result in one setting supports the same result in a second setting only if the settings are alike in the respect that produced the result. Numbered cloakroom tags cut losses where coats were lost at an unattended hook. The pool loses towels from unlocked lockers, and the hooks are not the problem.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The cloakroom result does not support expecting numbered tags to cut towel losses at the pool.","Numbered tags would increase towel losses at the pool.","The pool should remove its lockers.","Cloakroom tags are printed on thicker stock than pool tags.","Every unattended hook is in the cloakroom."],"a":0,"e":"The losses do not arise in the same way, so the likeness the principle requires is missing. The cloakroom result therefore does not support the transfer. The attractive choice — Numbered tags would increase towel losses at the pool. — loses because a missing likeness blocks support for a benefit. It does not predict harm. The attractive sentence invents an effect the principle does not license."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: Pointing only to a proponent's private advantage does not, by itself, establish that the proponent's recommendation is a bad one. The only remark against the lighting designer's proposal is that the designer would be paid extra to carry it out.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The lighting proposal is a good one.","The remark about extra pay does not, by itself, establish that the lighting proposal is a bad one.","The designer should refuse the extra pay.","Private advantage is the best evidence about lighting angles.","The lighting proposal has already been built."],"a":1,"e":"The only remark cites a private advantage, extra pay. The principle says that does not establish that the recommendation is bad. The conforming judgment stops at that failure. The attractive choice — The lighting proposal is a good one. — loses because a failed attack is not an endorsement. The proposal could still be bad for reasons nobody gave. The attractive sentence turns the silence of other criticisms into a finding that the design is good."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: If each part of a load is under a limit and the load is no heavier than its heaviest part, the load is under that limit. Each of the four bronze bells is under the tower crane's limit, and the set weighs no more than the heaviest bell.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["Each bell is under the limit only because the set is under the limit.","The tower crane can lift every bell in the city.","The set of four bells is under the tower crane's limit.","The heaviest bell should be left on the ground.","Bronze bells are quieter when lifted together."],"a":2,"e":"The facts supply both conditions of the principle: each bell is under the limit, and the set is no heavier than the heaviest bell. The set is therefore under the limit. The attractive choice — Each bell is under the limit only because the set is under the limit. — loses because the principle runs from the parts and the cap to the set. The attractive sentence reverses that order and is not a judgment the principle draws from these facts."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: Two changes that happen in the same season do not, by themselves, show that one of them produced the other. The only support offered for blaming a new bike corral for a drop in storefront vacancies is that both occurred last spring.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The bike corral did not produce the drop in vacancies.","Storefront vacancies fall only after a bike corral is installed.","The bike corral should be removed.","Last spring's overlap does not show that the bike corral produced the drop in vacancies.","The stores were vacant before the corral was proposed."],"a":3,"e":"The only support is that the two changes shared a season. The principle says that overlap does not show production. The judgment is a failure of proof. The attractive choice — The bike corral did not produce the drop in vacancies. — loses because a failure to show production is not a finding that the corral was innocent. Something unstated could still link them. The attractive sentence converts a gap into a negative verdict."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: A conditional grant of permission is not a requirement to act. Volunteers may handle the sealed charts if they wear cotton-free gloves. No volunteer is wearing cotton-free gloves today.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["No volunteer is permitted to handle the sealed charts today.","Every volunteer must wear cotton-free gloves tomorrow.","The sealed charts have already been handled.","Cotton-free gloves are required for every room in the building.","The glove rule does not require any volunteer to handle the sealed charts today."],"a":4,"e":"\"If they wear the gloves, they may handle the charts\" permits an action. It does not require it. Nobody is wearing the gloves, and the principle still does not turn the permission into a duty. The conforming judgment is that the rule requires no handling. The attractive choice — No volunteer is permitted to handle the sealed charts today. — loses because the facts may leave the volunteers short of the stated permission, but the principle given is about not converting permission into a requirement. \"No one is permitted\" is a different judgment, and it is not the one this principle delivers. The attractive ban answers a question the principle is not applying."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: If two words share a spelling and the argument treats them as one requirement, the argument has not been made when the words differ in meaning. The lease says \"quiet\" to mean no amplified music. The tenant's note says the apartment is quiet, meaning only that the refrigerator hum is low. The note is offered as proof that the lease is satisfied.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The note has not shown that the lease's requirement is satisfied.","The apartment violates the lease because a refrigerator is running.","The lease forbids refrigerators.","Every apartment in the building is quiet in both senses.","The tenant should be evicted for the note's wording."],"a":0,"e":"The two uses of \"quiet\" differ, and the note is treated as if it met the lease. The principle says the argument has not been made. The attractive choice — The apartment violates the lease because a refrigerator is running. — loses because a low hum is the tenant's meaning, not a proven breach of the music rule. The principle withholds credit from the note. It does not find a violation."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":3,"s":"Principle: A group's property follows from each member's property only when the group cannot exceed its greatest member in that property. Each crate in the shipment is under 40 pounds. Nothing is said about whether the shipment can weigh more than its heaviest crate.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The shipment is under 40 pounds.","It has not been shown that the shipment is under 40 pounds.","Each crate weighs exactly 40 pounds.","The shipment contains one crate.","A shipment over 40 pounds cannot be insured."],"a":1,"e":"The principle allows the group inference only with an extra cap. That cap is not given, so the shipment's weight has not been shown. The attractive choice — The shipment is under 40 pounds. — loses because the flashy arithmetic treats \"each under 40\" as \"all under 40 together.\" The principle says that move is unavailable until the cap is stated. The attractive sentence makes the move anyway."},
{"t":"Principle apply","pattern":"Applying a principle","d":2,"s":"Principle: If a speaker concludes that one of two stated options must occur, and a third option is available, the conclusion is not established. The producer concludes that the scene must be cut, because the only other option the producer stated is cancelling the preview, which the producer will not do. The scene could instead be shortened.","q":"Which one of the following judgments most closely conforms to the principle?","c":["The scene should be shortened.","The preview should be cancelled.","The producer has not established that the scene must be cut.","Every scene in the play is too long.","The producer has established that the scene must be cut."],"a":2,"e":"A third option, shortening, is available, and the producer treated two options as exhaustive. The principle says the conclusion that the scene must be cut is not established. The attractive choice — The scene should be shortened. — loses because an available third option blocks the producer's proof. It does not select that option as what should be done. The attractive sentence recommends a course the principle does not choose."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The tram poll questioned only last week's riders and is the only evidence offered. So it does not establish what most residents think of the tram.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["No transit decision is justified unless every resident has been interviewed.","Riders are the only people whose views about a tram matter.","A poll establishes a majority whenever most respondents agree.","A poll of a service's current users, by itself, does not establish the views of residents generally.","Last week's riders are a random sample of all residents."],"a":3,"e":"The argument needs a rule that blocks a users-only poll from speaking for residents. The current-users principle, applied to this poll, yields exactly the stated failure of proof. The attractive choice — No transit decision is justified unless every resident has been interviewed. — loses because a demand that every resident be interviewed is stronger than the argument. The argument claims only that this poll does not establish the residents' view, not that nothing can be decided until the whole city is asked."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"Commissioned scores fell as a share of the programs, and the number of programs stayed the same. So the orchestra played fewer commissioned scores.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["If a category's number falls, its share of the total falls.","An orchestra should commission fewer scores whenever audiences shrink.","Equal program counts show that two seasons were equally difficult.","A share can fall only if every other share rises by the same amount.","If a category's share of an unchanged total falls, the number of items in that category falls."],"a":4,"e":"The argument has a fixed total and a smaller share, and it infers a smaller count. The forward share-to-count rule makes that inference follow. The attractive choice — If a category's number falls, its share of the total falls. — loses because the reversed rule starts from a count the argument does not have and ends at a share the argument already used as a premise. It cannot justify the step that was actually taken."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The harpist cites no soil tests, and harp performance is unrelated to walls. That statement is the only ground offered. So it does not settle whether the retaining wall should be rebuilt.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["A statement whose only ground is expertise in an unrelated field does not settle a technical question.","A technical question should be settled against the recommendation of any non-engineer.","Harpists are reliable judges of audience size.","Soil tests are required only for new construction.","Any public statement settles the question it addresses."],"a":0,"e":"The argument moves from unrelated expertise, as the sole ground, to a failure to settle the wall question. The matching principle licenses that move and no more. The attractive choice — A technical question should be settled against the recommendation of any non-engineer. — loses because settling the question against the harpist would decide that the wall should not be rebuilt. The argument does not decide the wall. It decides only that this statement fails to settle it. The attractive principle is a stronger, opposite-facing rule."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"Any June overflow would have been entered, the June cistern log is complete, and it records none. So the cistern did not overflow in June.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["If a log records an event, the event occurred.","If any instance of an event would have been entered in a complete log and none is entered, the event did not occur in the period the log covers.","Cisterns overflow only in June.","A complete log is one that the keeper has signed.","Silence in a log is evidence only about the keeper's handwriting."],"a":1,"e":"The argument goes from \"would have been entered,\" completeness, and silence to \"did not occur.\" That is the principle that closes the inference. The attractive choice — If a log records an event, the event occurred. — loses because the reversed rule starts from an entry the log does not contain. It would help an argument that cited a recorded overflow. This argument cites the absence of one."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The only criticism of the cataloging plan is that the archivist wants a larger budget. So that criticism does not show the plan is unwise.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["A plan proposed by someone who wants a larger budget is a wise plan.","Budget motives are the best evidence about cataloging methods.","Criticism that cites only a person's motive does not, by itself, show that the person's plan is unwise.","No plan should be adopted if its author has a motive.","Archivists should not discuss budgets in public."],"a":2,"e":"The argument treats a motive-only criticism as failing to show unwisdom. The motive principle justifies that limited conclusion. The attractive choice — A plan proposed by someone who wants a larger budget is a wise plan. — loses because calling the plan wise endorses it. The argument does not endorse the plan. It says this criticism fails. The attractive principle answers a more flattering question."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The crosswalk and the sales rise are the only facts cited, and they occurred in the same month. So the rise has not been shown to be an effect of the crosswalk.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["If two events occur in the same month, neither caused the other.","Retail sales rise only after street paint dries.","A crosswalk causes a sales rise whenever merchants request one.","Joint occurrence, by itself, does not establish that one event caused the other.","Events in different months cannot be causally related."],"a":3,"e":"The argument has only the overlap and concludes that causation is not established. The modest principle justifies a gap in proof. The attractive choice — If two events occur in the same month, neither caused the other. — loses because the stronger principle would conclude that the crosswalk did not cause the rise. The argument stops at \"not shown.\" A rule that forbids causation whenever dates match is more than the reasoning uses, and it is not supported by the premise."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"Each bell is under the crane's limit, and the set weighs no more than the heaviest bell. So the set is under the crane's limit.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["If a group is under a limit, each member of the group is under that limit.","A crane can lift a set of bells only if the bells are bronze.","The heaviest member of a group is always the group itself.","No limit applies to a set of four objects.","If each member of a group is under a limit and the group weighs no more than its heaviest member, the group is under that limit."],"a":4,"e":"The argument uses both the individual weights and the cap at the heaviest bell. The principle that combines those two facts yields the group conclusion. The attractive choice — If a group is under a limit, each member of the group is under that limit. — loses because the reversed principle starts from the group, which is the conclusion, and derives the parts, which are already premises. It does not help the direction the argument travels."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The producer says the scene must be cut because the preview will not be cancelled, and those were the only options the producer stated. The scene could be shortened instead. So the producer has not established that the scene must be cut.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["If a third option is available, a conclusion that one of two stated options must occur is not established.","A scene should be shortened whenever cancelling a preview is undesirable.","Previews should never be cancelled.","A scene that can be shortened should be cut entirely.","Stating two options proves that no third option exists."],"a":0,"e":"Shortening is on the table, so the two-option conclusion is not established. The principle about an available third option justifies that result. The attractive choice — A scene should be shortened whenever cancelling a preview is undesirable. — loses because the argument does not recommend shortening. It says the producer's \"must cut\" is unproven. A rule that chooses shortening decides a question the argument leaves open."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"The lease's \"quiet\" means no amplified music. The tenant's \"quiet\" means a low refrigerator hum. The note treats the tenant's word as satisfying the lease. So the note has not shown that the lease is satisfied.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["A lease is satisfied only if every appliance in the apartment is silent.","When an argument treats two meanings of a word as one requirement, it has not established the requirement that uses the other meaning.","Words that are spelled alike always carry the same legal meaning.","Refrigerator hum is a form of amplified music.","A tenant's note is better evidence than a lease."],"a":1,"e":"The argument's point is the slide between two meanings. The principle about treating those meanings as one requirement yields the failure of proof. The attractive choice — A lease is satisfied only if every appliance in the apartment is silent. — loses because an appliance-silence rule would concern the hum itself. The argument does not claim the hum breaches the lease. It claims the note used the wrong meaning. The attractive principle changes the subject."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The pool loses towels from unlocked lockers, not from unattended hooks, which is how the cloakroom was losing coats. So the cloakroom's tagged-hook result does not support expecting tags to cut the pool's losses.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["A device that cuts losses in one setting will increase losses in every unlike setting.","Pools and cloakrooms should use the same tags.","A result supports the same result in a second setting only if the settings are alike in the respect that produced the result.","Unlocked lockers are the only way towels are lost anywhere.","A result in one setting never bears on any other setting."],"a":2,"e":"The argument refuses the transfer because the loss mechanism differs. The likeness principle justifies refusing the transfer. The attractive choice — A device that cuts losses in one setting will increase losses in every unlike setting. — loses because the argument does not predict that tags would make the pool worse. A rule that converts every failed analogy into harm is stronger than the reasoning, and it is the wrong direction."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":2,"s":"The next pump test uses a different fuel from the twenty successful diesel tests. So those tests do not support expecting the pump to start.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["An attempt under new conditions will fail.","Diesel is the only fuel a backup pump can use.","Twenty successes establish a permanent mechanical guarantee.","Past successes support expecting the next attempt only when the next attempt matches their conditions.","A change in fuel is irrelevant when the pump is the same machine."],"a":3,"e":"The fuel does not match, and the argument withholds the expectation. The matching-conditions principle justifies withholding it. The attractive choice — An attempt under new conditions will fail. — loses because the argument does not say the pump will fail. A principle that predicts failure under any new condition decides more than the unmatched record can decide."},
{"t":"Principle identify","pattern":"Identifying a principle","d":3,"s":"Volunteers may handle the sealed charts if they wear cotton-free gloves. The rule is being read as requiring someone to handle the charts today. That reading does not follow.","q":"Which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the reasoning?","c":["Volunteers must handle the sealed charts whenever cotton-free gloves are available.","Cotton-free gloves are the only gloves the archive owns.","A permission is valid only if it is exercised every day.","Sealed charts should remain sealed permanently.","A rule that permits an action under a condition does not, by itself, require anyone to perform the action."],"a":4,"e":"The argument rejects a reading that turns \"may\" into a duty. The permission-is-not-a-requirement principle justifies that rejection. The attractive choice — Volunteers must handle the sealed charts whenever cotton-free gloves are available. — loses because the attractive principle is the reading the argument says does not follow. It would justify the opposite conclusion, a duty to handle the charts, which is what the argument refuses."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The workshop that replaced its saws with a model advertised as quieter received more noise complaints the following month, not fewer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The complaint form was revised that month to ask specifically about saw noise, and the old form had no place to record it.","The new saws were advertised as quieter than the saws they replaced.","The workshop sits in a district zoned for light industry.","The new saws were ordered from a printed catalog.","Workers at the workshop already owned hearing protection."],"a":0,"e":"A form that newly asks about saw noise will collect complaints the old form could not record. More recorded complaints can then coexist with quieter saws. The discrepancy is about the count of complaints, and the form explains the count. The attractive choice — The new saws were advertised as quieter than the saws they replaced. — loses because the advertisement is the expectation that makes the rise surprising. Repeating it restates the puzzle. It does not explain why the complaint count went up."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The branch that extended its evening hours circulated fewer books that month than in the previous month, when it closed at supper time.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Longer opening hours give patrons more time in which to borrow books.","The evening hours replaced the morning hours, which had accounted for most of the branch's checkouts, and evening visitors mostly read on site.","The branch owns more novels than atlases.","Evening visitors enter through the same door as morning visitors.","The branch is on a bus line."],"a":1,"e":"Losing the morning, when most checkouts happened, can cut circulation even while the building stays open later for people who do not check books out. The hours moved; they did not simply grow. The attractive choice — Longer opening hours give patrons more time in which to borrow books. — loses because extra time to borrow is the reason one would have expected circulation to rise. It deepens the surprise instead of explaining the drop."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"After the fishery cut the commercial quota, the dock weighed in more commercial fish that season than in the previous, higher-quota season.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A lower quota is supposed to reduce the weight of fish landed commercially.","The dock is downriver from the spawning beds.","The old quota had been enforced on reported weight, and skippers underreported; the new season required a scale at the dock for every landing.","Commercial boats are painted different colors.","The quota is printed in the harbor handbook."],"a":2,"e":"If earlier landings were underreported and the new season weighs every landing, the recorded weight can rise while the legal quota falls. The discrepancy is in the books, and the scale explains the books. The attractive choice — A lower quota is supposed to reduce the weight of fish landed commercially. — loses because the point of a lower quota is the expectation that makes the heavier weigh-in odd. Restating that purpose does not reconcile the two seasons."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The lab that installed a faster centrifuge completed fewer sample runs the following week than with the slower machine.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A faster centrifuge finishes a sample run in less time than a slower one.","The lab is on the second floor.","Sample tubes are labeled with marker.","The faster centrifuge required a new rotor that arrived only on the fifth day, and the machine sat idle until the rotor was fitted.","The centrifuge plugs into a standard outlet."],"a":3,"e":"An idle machine completes nothing, however fast it would be once fitted. Four idle days can wipe out the gain from three fast days. The missing rotor explains the weekly count. The attractive choice — A faster centrifuge finishes a sample run in less time than a slower one. — loses because speed is why the new centrifuge was expected to raise the number of runs. Restating that specification describes the puzzle and does not mention the wait for the rotor."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The squadron that added a second daily drill session recorded more missed formations at the next review than when it drilled only once a day.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A second daily session provides more practice at holding a formation.","The review is held on a parade ground.","Formations are diagrammed in a pocket book.","The second session meets after lunch.","The second session was used to teach a new, denser formation, and the review was the first time that formation was called."],"a":4,"e":"The extra session introduced a harder formation that the review then called for the first time. More drill on a new pattern can still produce more misses than a familiar pattern received from one session. The programs are not the same test. The attractive choice — A second daily session provides more practice at holding a formation. — loses because extra practice is why the missed formations look surprising. It does not identify the change in what was being drilled."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The apartment house that added a night security guard reported more burglaries the next quarter than in the previous quarter, when the lobby was unstaffed overnight.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Before the guard was hired, burglaries were recorded only if a resident filed a police report; the guard logs every forced lock, including those residents never reported.","A night guard is present in order to deter burglaries.","The house is a six-story walk-up.","The guard wears a uniform supplied by the building.","Residents receive a key fob for the front door."],"a":0,"e":"The guard's log counts forced locks that used to go unreported. The recorded number can rise because the counting changed, even if intrusions fell. That reconciles the guard with the higher figure. The attractive choice — A night guard is present in order to deter burglaries. — loses because deterrence is the hope that makes the rise look backwards. Restating the guard's purpose does not explain the books."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The restaurant that added three servers had longer ticket times the following week than when it staffed the floor with fewer people.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Adding servers is a way to take orders and deliver plates sooner.","The new servers were trainees, and each trainee's orders had to be rebuilt by a senior server before they reached an unchanged kitchen.","The restaurant is on a corner.","Tickets are printed on white paper.","The kitchen has a single pass."],"a":1,"e":"Trainee orders that must be rebuilt consume the senior servers and still arrive at the same kitchen. More bodies on the floor can then slow the ticket rather than speed it. The training bottleneck explains the times. The attractive choice — Adding servers is a way to take orders and deliver plates sooner. — loses because faster service is the reason extra servers were expected to shorten tickets. Restating that purpose keeps the puzzle and does not mention the rebuilt orders."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The journal that started screening submissions with plagiarism software accepted a higher share of submissions the following year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Plagiarism software is used to reject submissions that copy other work.","The journal is published four times a year.","Authors who had been submitting copied work stopped sending it, and the software's rejections were outnumbered by the loss of those weak submissions from the denominator.","Submissions arrive by a web form.","The software was written by a university lab."],"a":2,"e":"If the copied papers leave the pool, the remaining submissions are stronger, and the acceptance share can rise even while the software rejects the copies that still arrive. The share is a ratio, and the pool changed. The attractive choice — Plagiarism software is used to reject submissions that copy other work. — loses because the software's rejecting function is why a higher acceptance share looks odd. Naming that function again does not show how the ratio could increase."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The bakery that switched to a more expensive butter sold fewer croissants the next month, despite keeping the sticker price the same.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A richer butter is widely said to make a croissant more attractive to buyers.","The bakery opens at dawn.","Croissants are displayed in a single case.","The new butter required a longer proof, the bakers kept the old schedule, and the underproofed croissants were often left in the case.","The more expensive butter comes from another county."],"a":3,"e":"An unchanged schedule with a dough that needs more time yields a worse pastry. Buyers can refuse it even at the old price. The quality drop explains the sales drop. The attractive choice — A richer butter is widely said to make a croissant more attractive to buyers. — loses because the reputation of richer butter is the reason sales were expected to hold or rise. It is the puzzling expectation, not a resolution."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The clinic that added appointment reminders by text had a higher no-show rate the next month than in the month before the texts began.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A reminder text gives a patient a prompt to attend the appointment.","The clinic is on the ground floor.","Reminder texts are sent the day before.","Patients check in at a single desk.","The texts began in the month the clinic also opened evening slots that many patients booked and then skipped, and daytime no-shows fell."],"a":4,"e":"The overall rate can rise because a new pool of evening bookings is skipped, even while daytime no-shows fall. The texts are not the only change, and the new slots carry the increase. The attractive choice — A reminder text gives a patient a prompt to attend the appointment. — loses because a prompt to attend is why the no-show rate was expected to fall. That expectation is the discrepancy, and repeating it does not separate evening slots from daytime ones."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The city that planted more street trees recorded a higher summer peak temperature on its official downtown sensor than in the previous, less-planted summer.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The official sensor was moved that spring from a shaded courtyard to the middle of an unshaded asphalt plaza, and the trees were planted on side streets.","Street trees are planted in part because their shade can cool nearby air.","The city owns the downtown sensor.","Side streets are narrower than the avenue.","The trees were planted in spring."],"a":0,"e":"The sensor left the shade and now sits on hot asphalt, while the new trees shade other streets. The official number can rise even if the side streets cooled. The discrepancy is an artifact of the move. The attractive choice — Street trees are planted in part because their shade can cool nearby air. — loses because cooling is the reason a higher official peak looks wrong. Restating the horticultural purpose does not mention where the sensor went."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The trail crew that replaced uneven stepping stones with a smoother walking surface recorded more twisted ankles the next month.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A smoother walking surface is installed to reduce the chance of a twisted ankle.","The new surface was laid on a steeper pitch than the stones, and the twisted ankles occurred on the wet descent, which the gapped stones had shed.","The trail is in a county park.","The crew marks completed work with tape.","Stepping stones are quarried nearby."],"a":1,"e":"The smooth surface sits on a steeper, slower-drying descent. Ankles can twist there more often than on rough stones that shed water, even though each step feels smoother. The pitch and the water explain the injuries. The attractive choice — A smoother walking surface is installed to reduce the chance of a twisted ankle. — loses because reducing twists is the reason a smoother surface was expected to help. Restating that purpose describes the puzzle and says nothing about the steeper wet descent."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The archive that began storing manuscripts in a cooler vault found more mold on the manuscripts the following year.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Cooler air slows the growth of many molds.","The vault is in the basement.","The cooler vault was also more humid, and the manuscripts had previously been kept in a warmer room that stayed dry.","Manuscripts are labeled with pencil.","The archive employs a conservator."],"a":2,"e":"Humidity can dominate temperature. A cool damp vault can grow more mold than a warm dry room. The temperature fell and the moisture rose, which resolves the inspection. The attractive choice — Cooler air slows the growth of many molds. — loses because the slowing effect of cool air is the reason the mold count looks backwards. Naming that effect again leaves the humidity out and keeps the puzzle intact."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The clinic that opened a second pharmacy window filled fewer prescriptions per hour than when it staffed a single window.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["A second window gives patients another place to hand in a prescription.","The pharmacy is next to the lobby.","Prescription slips are white.","The second window was staffed by pulling the only pharmacist off the filling bench, and prescriptions cannot be released without that pharmacist's check.","The clinic opens at seven."],"a":3,"e":"The window was gained by abandoning the bench that actually fills and checks. Patients can hand slips in faster and still wait longer for a release. Hourly completions fall because the check moved. The attractive choice — A second window gives patients another place to hand in a prescription. — loses because another hand-in point is why a second window was expected to raise the hourly count. It does not mention removing the pharmacist from the bench."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":2,"s":"The law firm that started billing in six-minute increments, in order to capture small tasks, billed fewer hours the next quarter than under its old quarter-hour minimum.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["Six-minute increments record work that a quarter-hour minimum rounds away.","The firm has a downtown office.","Invoices are sent on the first of the month.","Associates record time on a computer.","Clients who saw the small tasks itemized began doing those tasks themselves, and those tasks had been a large part of the old minimums."],"a":4,"e":"Itemizing the small tasks drove clients to take them in-house. The firm lost a body of work that the old minimums had been padding. Finer increments can then total fewer billed hours. The attractive choice — Six-minute increments record work that a quarter-hour minimum rounds away. — loses because the rounding explanation is why finer increments were expected to raise the total. It restates the firm's plan and does not mention the clients' reaction."},
{"t":"Paradox","pattern":"Resolving a discrepancy","d":3,"s":"The school that banned phones from classrooms recorded lower scores on the same end-of-term test than in the previous year, when phones were allowed.","q":"Which one of the following, if true, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?","c":["The ban year was also the year the school shortened the term by three weeks, and the test covered the full syllabus.","Phones in a classroom are a common source of distraction during lessons.","The test is given in the gym.","Classrooms have windows.","The school day starts at eight."],"a":0,"e":"A shorter term with the same full syllabus leaves less teaching time. Scores can fall for that reason even if the phone ban removed a distraction. The calendar change reconciles the scores. The attractive choice — Phones in a classroom are a common source of distraction during lessons. — loses because distraction is why the ban was expected to raise scores. Repeating it describes the puzzle. It does not mention the lost weeks."}
]);
